---
components: ["PasswordStrength", "PasswordRequirements"]
description: Password strength shows how guessable a password is as it is typed, and a checklist shows which of your rules it meets. Strength is advice; the checklist is the policy.
keywords: ["password meter", "password checklist", "password policy", "zxcvbn"]
---

<script>
  import { Stack, PasswordStrength, PasswordRequirements, passwordRules } from "carbon-components-svelte";
</script>

## Basic

Place a `PasswordStrength` after a `PasswordInput` and pass it the same `value`. Point the input's `aria-describedby` at the strength's `id` so a screen reader hears it on focus. The bar fills one more segment at each of four levels: weak, fair, good, and strong.

Call `flush()` from the input's `blur` event to announce a pending level at once. While typing, the level is announced only after a pause.

<FileSource src="/framed/PasswordStrength/PasswordStrengthBasic" />

## Usage

| Do | Why |
| --- | --- |
| Show strength as advice and requirements as the rule. | A meter cannot block a submit. Only a requirement can, and a server must check the same rule again. |
| Prefer length and a blocklist to composition rules. | NIST SP 800-63B and the UK government guidance both advise against forced numbers, symbols and case, which push people to `Password1!`. |
| Allow spaces and paste. | Passphrases and password managers need both. Add `noSpaces()` only for a secret that goes into a header or a shell. |
| Pass the person's email and name as `userInputs`. | A password built from them is easy to guess, and the estimator only knows to penalize it if you say so. |
| Treat the result as guidance, not security. | The estimator has no dictionary of words, so it cannot spot every predictable phrase. See [Guidance, not security](#guidance-not-security). |

## Levels

Each level fills one more segment and names itself in text, so the level never depends on color. Set `levelTexts` to translate the names.

<Stack gap={6}>
  <PasswordStrength value="password" />
  <PasswordStrength value="Tr0ub4dor&3" />
  <PasswordStrength value="j8Kd2nQ9vT5wZ3" />
  <PasswordStrength value="correct horse battery staple" />
</Stack>

### Empty

With no value, the bar is empty and the level is "None". Nothing is announced.

<PasswordStrength value="" />

## Sizes

Set `size` to control the height of the bar. The default is `"md"`.

### Medium

<PasswordStrength value="Tr0ub4dor&3" size="md" />

### Small

<PasswordStrength value="Tr0ub4dor&3" size="sm" />

## Feedback

Below the bar, the estimator says what made the password weak and what to try. Feedback shows for weak and fair passwords and is hidden once a password is good. Set `showFeedback` to `false` to hide it, or `feedbackTexts` to replace the text. Keys are the codes the estimator returns, such as `top10`, `sequence`, and `addWords`.

<Stack gap={6}>
  <PasswordStrength value="qwertyuiop" />
  <PasswordStrength value="abcdefgh1234" />
  <PasswordStrength value="aaaaaaaaaaaa" />
  <PasswordStrength value="AliceSmith1990" userInputs={["alice.smith@example.com"]} />
</Stack>

### Translated

Set `levelTexts`, `valueText` and `feedbackTexts` for another language. `valueText` builds the `aria-valuetext` and the announcement from the level text.

<PasswordStrength
  value="password"
  labelText="Robustesse du mot de passe"
  levelTexts={{ weak: "Faible" }}
  valueText={(level) => `Robustesse : ${level}`}
  feedbackTexts={{ top10: "Ce mot de passe figure parmi les 10 plus courants." }}
/>

## Requirements

A `PasswordRequirements` checks a list of rules and shows each as met or not met, with an icon and text. It binds `valid`, and `unmet` for the error you show on submit. The default rules follow NIST SP 800-63B: a minimum length and a blocklist, with no composition rules. A rule that checks the person's own words appears only once `userInputs` is set.

<FileSource src="/framed/PasswordStrength/PasswordRequirementsBasic" />

### Composition rules

If your policy requires them, build the list with `passwordRules`. Each rule takes the text to show as its last argument.

<FileSource src="/framed/PasswordStrength/PasswordRequirementsComposition" />

| Rule | Checks |
| --- | --- |
| `minLength(n)` | At least `n` characters, counting each Unicode code point once. |
| `hasNumber()` | A digit in any script. |
| `hasSymbol()` | A character that is not a letter, a digit or whitespace. |
| `noSpaces()` | No whitespace. NIST says to allow spaces, so use this only where they break the secret. |
| `notCommon()` | Not on the blocklist, allowing a few trailing characters and letter swaps. |
| `notContainingUserInput()` | Contains none of the `userInputs`. |
| `matches(other)` | Equals another value, for a confirm field. |

### Custom

A rule is an object with an `id`, the `text` to show, and a `test` that receives the password. Add `when` to leave a rule out.

<FileSource src="/framed/PasswordStrength/PasswordRequirementsCustom" />

### Invalid

Set `invalid` after a failed submit to mark every unmet rule with an error icon. The input's own error should list what is unmet. Build it from `unmet`.

<PasswordRequirements
  value="hunter2"
  invalid
  requirements={[passwordRules.minLength(12), passwordRules.hasNumber(), passwordRules.notCommon()]}
/>

## Examples

### Sign up

Strength, the checklist, and a confirm field. Submit is blocked until every requirement is met and the two passwords match.

<FileSource src="/framed/PasswordStrength/PasswordSignUp" />

### Change password

The new password must differ from the current one. A custom rule checks it, and the current password also counts as a word to avoid.

<FileSource src="/framed/PasswordStrength/PasswordChange" />

### API key policy

A secret that is pasted into a header or an env file must have no spaces, and its length matters more than anything else. This example turns composition rules off and requires 32 characters.

<FileSource src="/framed/PasswordStrength/PasswordApiKeyPolicy" />

## Blocklist

The estimator and `notCommon()` compare against the 1,000 most common passwords, which adds about 3.6 kB gzipped. Load the 10,000 most common for a stricter check. It is about 23 kB gzipped, decodes in about 2 ms, and looks a word up in well under a microsecond. Import it only where you need it, and pass it as `blocklist`.

Pass your own list as an array of strings, most common first.

<FileSource src="/framed/PasswordStrength/PasswordStrengthBlocklist" />

## Custom estimator

Set `estimate` to replace the built-in estimator with zxcvbn-ts or a call to your server. It receives the password and `userInputs`, and returns a result with a `score` from 0 to 4 and optional `feedback`. It may return a promise.

The component waits `estimateDelay` after the last keystroke, drops a result that arrives after the password changed again, and aborts the `signal` of the call it supersedes. While a call is pending, the last result stays on screen and the meter has `aria-busy`. If the call throws, the built-in estimate is shown and `error` is dispatched.

<FileSource src="/framed/PasswordStrength/PasswordStrengthAsync" />

With zxcvbn-ts, which is about 800 kB of dictionaries, load it only when needed:

```svelte
<script>
  import { PasswordStrength } from "carbon-components-svelte";

  async function estimate(password, { userInputs }) {
    const { zxcvbn, zxcvbnOptions } = await import("@zxcvbn-ts/core");
    // Set zxcvbnOptions.dictionary, graphs and translations once.
    return zxcvbn(password, userInputs);
  }
</script>

<PasswordStrength {value} {estimate} />
```

The `feedback` of zxcvbn-ts holds translated sentences, which are shown as they are.

## Privacy

The password is read and never stored, logged, or put in an event or in the result of an estimate. The `result` binds a score, a count of guesses, and the positions of the patterns found, never the text. To keep it that way:

| Do | Why |
| --- | --- |
| Check a password against a breach list with k-anonymity. | Send only the first five characters of its SHA-1 hash to the Pwned Passwords range API, and compare the rest on your side. The service never sees the password or the full hash. |
| Run the check from your server if you can. | A browser call exposes the request to extensions and to your analytics. |
| Keep the password out of error reports and session replays. | Mask the input in your replay tool, and do not put it in a log line. |

```js
async function pwned(password) {
  const bytes = new TextEncoder().encode(password);
  const digest = await crypto.subtle.digest("SHA-1", bytes);
  const hash = [...new Uint8Array(digest)]
    .map((b) => b.toString(16).padStart(2, "0"))
    .join("")
    .toUpperCase();
  const response = await fetch(
    `https://api.pwnedpasswords.com/range/${hash.slice(0, 5)}`,
    { headers: { "Add-Padding": "true" } },
  );
  const lines = (await response.text()).split("\n");
  const count = lines
    .map((line) => line.trim().split(":"))
    .find(([suffix]) => suffix === hash.slice(5));
  return count ? Number(count[1]) : 0;
}
```

Use it as a requirement. A rule is synchronous, so keep the count in state and write a rule that reads it, or return it from a custom `estimate`.

## Guidance, not security

The strength meter is a hint for the person typing, not a control. It runs in the browser, so anyone can bypass it, and the server must enforce the rules that matter.

The estimator counts guesses the way zxcvbn does: it finds the cheapest way to spell the password from common passwords, your `userInputs`, repeats, sequences, keyboard walks, dates, and what is left. It has no dictionary of English words, so it rates words not on the blocklist at about 2.5 bits per character. Passphrases of common words come out stronger than they are, and random lowercase letters come out weaker. The levels follow guesses at a slow hash: weak under 2^30, fair under 2^40, good under 2^50. Use a custom estimator if you need more.

## Accessibility

- The strength is a `role="meter"` named by its label, with `aria-valuetext` such as "Strength: Weak". It is not a tab stop.
- The checklist is a list. Each item holds its text and, visually hidden, ", met" or ", not met", so the state never depends on the icon or its color. Met, not met and error icons differ in shape.
- Neither component announces on every keystroke. Each speaks after typing pauses, and only when the message changed: the level and the reason, and the count of requirements met. Their live regions sit outside the element the input is described by.
- Point the input's `aria-describedby` at the `id` of the strength and the checklist. A screen reader reads the rules and the level when the field is focused.
- On a failed submit, list the unmet requirements in the input's `invalidText`, and set `invalid` on the checklist.
- In forced colors, a filled segment is solid and an empty one is an outline.
